---
// Tiny Planet 的介紹頁。
//
// 存在的理由有三個,缺一都還是會做:
//   1. 站上原本每頁只有 50~90 字,AdSense 最常見的退件理由就是低價值內容
//   2. 「browser flight game」「low poly planet」這類搜尋詞現在完全收不到
//   3. 對外推廣時需要一個能貼的落地頁,不能每次都直接丟遊戲網址
//
// 內容全部照實寫 —— 地標清單、玩法、做法都跟程式裡的實際狀況對得上。
import SubpageLayout from '../layouts/SubpageLayout.astro';

const LANDMARKS = [
  'Statue of Liberty and the Vessel, New York', 'Golden Gate Bridge, San Francisco',
  'Sydney Opera House', 'Eiffel Tower, Paris', 'Big Ben and the London Eye',
  "Saint Basil's Cathedral, Moscow", 'Leaning Tower of Pisa',
  'Kinderdijk windmills, Netherlands', 'Christ the Redeemer, Rio de Janeiro',
  'Taj Mahal', 'Angkor Wat', 'Hagia Sophia, Istanbul', 'Dome of the Rock, Jerusalem',
  'Taipei 101', 'Burj Khalifa, Dubai', 'Shanghai skyline', 'Colosseum, Rome',
  'Pyramids and Great Sphinx of Giza', 'Tokyo Tower', 'Machu Picchu',
  'Chichén Itzá', 'Great Wall of China', 'Sagrada Família, Barcelona',
  'Merdeka 118, Kuala Lumpur', 'Lotte World Tower, Seoul', 'Mecca Clock Tower',
  'Marina Bay Sands, Singapore', 'Forbidden City, Beijing', 'Petra, Jordan',
  'Edinburgh Castle', 'Uluru, Australia', 'Parthenon, Athens',
  'Mount Fuji and its lakeside torii', 'Neuschwanstein Castle', 'Alhambra, Granada',
  'Bagan temple plain, Myanmar', 'Tikal, Guatemala', 'Stonehenge',
  'Moai of Easter Island', 'Louvre Pyramid, Paris', 'Arc de Triomphe, Paris',
  'Santorini blue domes', 'Antarctic and Arctic research stations',
];

// 遊戲支援的 ?goto= 捷徑,跟 index.html 的 PLACES 對應
const SHORTCUTS = [
  ['ny', 'New York'], ['sf', 'Golden Gate Bridge'], ['paris', 'Eiffel Tower'],
  ['london', 'Big Ben'], ['rome', 'Colosseum'], ['giza', 'Pyramids'],
  ['athens', 'Parthenon'], ['barcelona', 'Sagrada Família'],
  ['edinburgh', 'Edinburgh Castle'], ['sydney', 'Opera House'],
  ['tokyo', 'Tokyo Tower'], ['machu', 'Machu Picchu'],
];
---
<SubpageLayout title="Tiny Planet — Fly Around a Whole World in Your Browser"
                current="tiny-planet"
                brand="X-Arts" brandAccent="Games" icon="assets/favicon.png">
  <div class="wrap"><div class="legal card">

    <h1>Tiny <span class="grad">Planet</span></h1>
    <div class="upd">A free browser flight sandbox · No download, no account</div>

    <p><a class="mail" href="/tiny-planet/"><b>▶ Play Tiny Planet now</b></a> — it runs in any
    modern browser on desktop, tablet, or phone. Nothing to install and nothing to sign up for.</p>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Tiny Planet is a small, calm flight game. You fly a paper biplane around an entire planet —
    not a level, not an arena, but a closed sphere you can circle in a few minutes. There is no
    score, no timer, and no way to lose. You take off and go wherever you like.</p>
    <p>The planet is small enough that the horizon curves visibly in front of you. Climb a little
    and you can see the far side coming over the edge; drop low and the ground rushes past between
    the trees. That sense of scale is the whole point of the game.</p>

    <h2>How to play</h2>
    <p>New players are flying within one click. Press <b>Take off</b> and you are already in the air —
    the plane flies itself forward, so you only ever steer.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>W / S</b> or <b>↑ / ↓</b> — pitch: dive toward the ground or climb away from it</li>
      <li><b>A / D</b> or <b>← / →</b> — roll: bank left and right to turn</li>
      <li><b>Shift</b> — boost</li>
      <li><b>Space</b> — fire a paintball</li>
      <li><b>Mouse drag</b> — look around without changing course</li>
      <li><b>Touch</b> — drag anywhere to steer, tap the button in the corner to shoot</li>
    </ul>
    <p>There is no tutorial to sit through and nothing to unlock. Every part of the planet is
    reachable from the moment you start.</p>

    <h2>Real places, in their real locations</h2>
    <p>The planet is built from real Earth elevation and land-cover data, so the continents are
    where you expect them to be. Forty-three landmarks are placed at their true coordinates —
    fly east from New York and you will cross the Atlantic and reach Europe, exactly as you would
    on the real globe.</p>
    <p>Because the planet is scaled down, one flight unit is roughly 23 km. A full lap takes a few
    minutes, which means you can pass a dozen countries in the time it takes to finish a coffee.</p>
    <p>The landmarks you can find:</p>
    <ul>
      {LANDMARKS.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Jump straight to a place</h2>
    <p>Add <code>?goto=</code> to the game's address to start somewhere specific. For example,
    <a class="mail" href="/tiny-planet/?goto=rome">/tiny-planet/?goto=rome</a> starts you over Italy.</p>
    <ul>
      {SHORTCUTS.map(([k, name]) => (
        <li><code>?goto={k}</code> — {name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>You can also pass raw coordinates, like <code>?goto=48.86,2.29</code>, to start anywhere on
    the planet.</p>

    <h2>Weather, day and night</h2>
    <p>The planet keeps its own time. Fly long enough and you will watch the sun go down, the sky
    turn orange behind the clouds, and the city lights switch on one district at a time. Rain
    sweeps across the ocean in bands you can fly into and out of. None of it is scripted — the
    weather and the day cycle run on their own whether you are watching or not.</p>

    <h2>Flying with other people</h2>
    <p>Other real players share the same sky. There are no lobbies, no matchmaking, and no waiting
    room: if someone else is flying, you will simply see their plane. Everyone carries a paintball
    gun, and a hit leaves a splash of colour on the other plane's wings. There is no health, no
    damage, and no respawn — it is just something to do when you pass someone.</p>
    <p>No account is needed and no names are shown. Nothing about your session is stored on our
    servers; see the <a class="mail" href="site-privacy.html">website privacy policy</a> for details.</p>

    <h2>How it was made</h2>
    <p>The planet is generated rather than modelled by hand. Terrain height comes from real
    elevation data mapped onto a subdivided icosphere, and the colour of every triangle is sampled
    from satellite land-cover imagery, which is why deserts, forests, and ice caps land in the
    right places without anyone painting them.</p>
    <p>The landmarks are a mix. Most are built procedurally in Blender from simple solids —
    good for anything with regular repeating structure, like the lattice of the Eiffel Tower or the
    stepped tiers of a skyscraper, where clean geometry beats detail. Others are generated as 3D
    meshes and then cleaned up, decimated, and oriented by hand — better for single sculptural
    forms like the Moai, Christ the Redeemer, or the Great Sphinx, where the silhouette is what
    makes them recognisable.</p>
    <p>Everything runs in WebGL through Three.js, with geometry compressed so the whole planet
    loads in a few seconds. There is no game engine and no build step behind it.</p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>Nothing. Tiny Planet is free, has no adverts inside the game itself, sells nothing, and
    asks for no account. If you enjoy it there is a donation link on the title screen, and that is
    the only money involved anywhere.</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p><b>Do I need to download anything?</b> No. It runs in the browser.</p>
    <p><b>Does it work on a phone?</b> Yes — drag to steer, tap to shoot. A larger screen makes the
    horizon easier to read, but nothing is missing on mobile.</p>
    <p><b>Can I lose or crash?</b> No. Fly into the ground and you bounce back up. It is meant to be
    something you can leave running.</p>
    <p><b>Is there an end?</b> No. The planet is the whole game.</p>
    <p><b>What languages does it support?</b> Ten, detected automatically from your browser:
    English, 繁體中文, 简体中文, 日本語, 한국어, Français, Italiano, Español, ไทย, and العربية.</p>

    <p><a class="mail" href="/tiny-planet/"><b>▶ Play Tiny Planet</b></a></p>

  </div></div>
</SubpageLayout>
